'''''Dalewa''''' - ''dale'', "speech", and ''awa'', "realm", "the realm of speech" - is the traditional script of [[Hachinawa]], in use for [[Hachina language|Hachina]] from about 700 CE. It is logographic, with characters standing for words rather than sounds, and it was taught in the temple establishments together with the calendar and the numerals.

From the sixteenth century it was displaced from official use by the [[Zong script in Hachinawa|Zong script]]. It survived in the temples, in the records of the priesthood and in the writing of the traditionalists. It is treated with the other scripts of the state in [[Scripts of Hachinawa]].

The traditional script, Dalewa, is logographic, with characters standing for words or for parts of words, and it is written with a numeral system of its own. It appeared about 700 CE, within two generations of the unification of the plain, and it appeared with the calendar rather than before it.

The name is the ordinary word for speech with the ordinary word for realm attached to it, and it carries the same construction as the state's own name, ''Hachinawa''. The script is not named for its inventor, its material or its purpose but for what it is: the realm in which the language is written, as the state is the realm in which the people live.

Its earliest surviving use is administrative. The oldest inscriptions are granary records: quantities of grain received, stored and issued, with the dates on which they moved. The calendar and the script were developed together because the state required both, one to fix the dates of sowing and harvest and the other to record the yield.

The script was taught in the temple establishments from its invention. The schools that produced the officials who measured grain and kept the tribute rolls, and the priests who kept the calendar, were institutions of the script as well as of the calendar, and the two competences were not distinguished in the curriculum.

== Structure ==

''Dalewa'' is logographic. A character, tsin, stands for a word rather than for a sound, and is read as the Hachina word for the thing it denotes. The script is therefore not a means of writing speech phonetically: a reader who knows the characters and not the language can recover the meaning of a text without being able to pronounce it.

Characters fall into four kinds.

* Pictorial tsin are derived from drawings of the thing denoted. ''Hacha'', "grain", is a stalk with the head bent; ''ku'', "water", is a vertical line with two strokes crossing it; ''tala'', "city", is a rectangle with a gap in one wall.
* Compound tsin are formed by combining two pictorial characters, and their meaning is that of the combination rather than either part. ''Hina'', "river", is ''ku'', "water", written beside a character for movement; ''washakaya'', "marshy plain", is ''washa'', "standing water", over ''kaya'', "open ground".
* Classifier tsin are semantic marks prefixed to another character to fix which of several senses of that character is meant. The classifier for people, a short vertical stroke with a dot, distinguishes ''kowa'', "people", from the same character read as "many"; the classifier for grain distinguishes ''hacha'', "maize", from the general character for plants.
* Borrowed tsin are characters used for their sound rather than their meaning, to write words that have no character of their own, including foreign names. Borrowed use is rare in official writing and was disliked by the temples, which kept the older readings.

