== Written on stone, then on paper ==

For seven centuries the ordinary medium of ''Dalewa'' was stone. The script was cut into monuments, boundary stones, granary walls and the walls of temples, and the record it produced was accordingly public, durable and brief: it suited the proclamation and the dedication rather than the ledger.

Paper reached Hachinawa through the Zong trade in the fifteenth century, and the effect on the administration was immediate. A script already seven centuries old could be written in quantity for the first time, and the record became voluminous, portable and disposable. The tribute registers, grain accounts, land registers and correspondence of the sixteenth century are consequences of a change of material rather than of method.

== Numerals ==

''Dalewa'' has a decimal numeral system with ten distinct signs and a positional notation, the position being marked by a stroke beneath the figure rather than by a zero.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Value !! Sign
|-
| one || a single upright stroke
|-
| two || two upright strokes joined at the base
|-
| three || three upright strokes joined at the base
|-
| four || a square
|-
| five || a square with a stroke through it
|-
| six || a square with two strokes at its upper corner
|-
| seven || a square with three strokes at its upper corner
|-
| eight || two squares side by side
|-
| nine || two squares with a stroke through the pair
|-
| ten || a single square with a stroke beneath it
|}

Numbers are written with the highest place first and the places separated by a space, so that ''two hundred and forty-one'' is written as the character for two, then a space, then four, then one.

== Calendar notation ==

Dates are written in three parts, in the order year, month, day, and are introduced by the character for the year in which the king's reign began rather than by a continuous count.

A date therefore takes the form: the reign-character, the number of the year of the reign, the month, the day. The months are the agricultural months of the calendar rather than named months, and are written with numerals.

The system means that a date cannot be read without knowing which king reigned, and that the record of a reign is the record of the years on which documents are dated. It is one of the reasons the royal lists of Hachinawa are as full as they are, and one of the reasons a document of an unrecorded king is difficult to place.

== Sample inscription ==

The following is a granary record of the kind on which the script's earliest use is attested, given line by line in transliteration with the meaning beneath. It is a reconstruction of the ordinary form rather than the transcription of one surviving stone.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Transliteration !! Meaning
|-
| kayahik tsu-hacha hik-na-tsu || In the reign of Kayahik, in the year nine of his reign,
|-
| hacha pah-tsap kan-tsula || the grain of the upland fields,
|-
| ku-tsin hacha cha-pach || measured by the grain measure,
|-
| hik-tsula awa-ha hacha hachin || received into the granary of the king,
|-
| hacha pach-mu hik-na-tsu || issued two hundred in the year nine.
|}

The record is dated by the reign, measures grain in a unit of the state's own definition, and names the granary by the king to whom it belonged. It is an account rather than a narrative, and the earliest use of ''Dalewa'' is of this kind.

